  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 338 AM EDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nWhile zonal flow will aloft will continue on Wednesday, a broader \npattern shift will begin to take place across most of the CONUS. The \nupper ridge out west will amplify through the early portions of the \nweekend as the upper trough over the eastern portions of the \ncountry begins to dig southward. The initial digging of this trough \nwill come late Wednesday into Thursday as an embedded shortwave and \nsurface cold front moves towards the Ohio Valley. Conditions over \nsouthern Indiana and central Kentucky will be dry for the daytime \nhours, with highs in the mid to upper 80s forecast north of the \nParkways, and places getting into the low 90s south. Chances for \nshowers and storms will increase Wednesday night, with widespread \n45-65% POPs forecast. There is still uncertainty in the guidance as \nto whether these storms will line up with the best instability and \nwind shear, so intensity of these storms is still uncertain.\n\nThe broad shortwave will slowly move through the region, finally \ndeparting to the east by Friday. As a result, chances for showers \nand storms (30-50%) will linger from late Wednesday night into early \nFriday. Available moisture will slowly decrease as the previously-\nmentioned frontal boundary pushes southward, exiting out of the area \nby Thursday night. LREF PW of ~1.8\" across the area late Wednesday \nwill decrease to less than 1.4\" by Friday morning, which should help \nlimit the extent of persistent heavy rainfall. With the multiple \nrounds of showers and storms, as well as the passing cold front, \nafternoon temperatures will remain in the 80s Thursday and Friday, \nwith morning lows in the upper 60s Thursday and low to mid 60s \nFriday.\n\nThe axis of the upper trough and an additional shortwave will move \nover the region by the latter half of the weekend, though \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance shows varying outputs on the \ntiming and progression of these features. Lower end chances for \nshowers and storms will accompany these features, but once the axis \nand shortwave moves through, calmer conditions are likely to follow. \nAfternoon highs will be in the 80s, with morning lows in the 60s \nSaturday and SUnday.",
